WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.545
[MUSIC]

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:14.489
Great leaders are committed to
developing other people as leaders,

00:00:14.489 --> 00:00:18.366
which often means putting them in
positions where they might fail.

00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:21.548
Encouraging them to step
outside their comfort zone and

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:26.016
helping them learn from setbacks and
mistakes as opposed to punishing them for

00:00:26.016 --> 00:00:28.193
setbacks and mistakes immediately.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:33.114
[MUSIC]

00:00:33.114 --> 00:00:38.738
Great leaders also really internalize the
responsibility to embody the culture and

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.935
to walk the talk.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:45.104
It's a truism in organizational life
that sort of the values plaque,

00:00:45.104 --> 00:00:49.058
the principles that we put up
on the wall is kind of BS.

00:00:49.058 --> 00:00:53.622
And I think that's because in a lot
of circumstances people see a big gap

00:00:53.622 --> 00:00:57.740
between the plaque on the wall and
the leaders actual behavior.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.582
They are finely tuned to have moments
where the leader is not acting

00:01:02.582 --> 00:01:05.359
in alignment with those principles.

00:01:05.359 --> 00:01:09.379
And so I think great leadership really
means committing to understanding those

00:01:09.379 --> 00:01:11.240
principles, internalizing them.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.001
And being very deliberate about following
through on them, especially when they

00:01:16.001 --> 00:01:20.074
don't align with what the leader
believes to be their self interest.

00:01:20.074 --> 00:01:23.950
If a leader is going to follow the values
on the plaque on the wall only when it's

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.949
personally expedient, I'd say that
creates, very toxic environment in which

00:01:27.949 --> 00:01:31.279
people realize those values on
the wall are not going to be followed.

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:35.067
They're not ones that we
need to listen to either.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:39.277
[MUSIC]

00:01:39.277 --> 00:01:43.698
A theme that comes up a lot in my practice
is that leaders occupy a particularly

00:01:43.698 --> 00:01:45.270
important symbolic role.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.582
And I think this is often neglected
in contemporary organizational life.

00:01:49.582 --> 00:01:52.145
We tend to think of leaders
as decision makers,

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.653
we think of leaders in
a very strategic sense.

00:01:54.653 --> 00:01:58.085
They're going to determine
the course of the organization,

00:01:58.085 --> 00:02:02.582
and they're going to set strategy, and
they're going to allocate resources.

00:02:02.582 --> 00:02:04.490
That's all true.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.068
It's also very cognitive.

00:02:05.068 --> 00:02:08.576
I think what that leaves out is
the emotional impact that a leader has as

00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:10.310
the symbolic leader of the tribe.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.186
The symbolic dimension of organizational
life is one that comes up quite a lot in

00:02:15.186 --> 00:02:16.073
my practice.

00:02:16.073 --> 00:02:19.896
People join organizations and feel
committed to organizations to a certain

00:02:19.896 --> 00:02:24.152
extent because of the feeling of community
that they enjoy among their colleagues.

00:02:24.152 --> 00:02:28.854
And the commitment that they feel from the
leader to sort of invest in the health and

00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:31.185
development growth of that community.

00:02:31.185 --> 00:02:35.606
It doesn't mean that a leader is
obligated to solve for those factors, but

00:02:35.606 --> 00:02:40.451
recognizing the leader bears a unique
responsibility to help that community feel

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:43.069
a sense of collective identity and growth.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:48.357
[MUSIC]

00:02:48.357 --> 00:02:53.943
Great leaders are also able to make
a distinction between leading and doing.

00:02:53.943 --> 00:02:58.218
Many of the people I work with
have a technical background,

00:02:58.218 --> 00:03:03.360
either as coders or developers,
as investors or financial analysts.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.931
And they've achieved their leadership
position by virtue of their technical

00:03:07.931 --> 00:03:08.560
prowess.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.839
And that is often very useful in the early
stages of a company's development because

00:03:12.839 --> 00:03:16.589
other people are looking to leader for
that kind of technical expertise.

00:03:16.589 --> 00:03:20.128
And yet if a leader remains
stuck in the process of doing,

00:03:20.128 --> 00:03:22.797
if they are essentially the doer in chief.

00:03:22.797 --> 00:03:27.638
If their leadership rests on their ability
to be the most effective person at that

00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:32.567
particular technical discipline or the
proverbial smartest person in the room.

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:36.740
Then they're going to cap
the organization's ability to grow.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.862
They will inhibit the organization's
capacity to scale.

00:03:39.862 --> 00:03:46.337
So a transitional phase that I help
a lot of leaders navigate through is

00:03:46.337 --> 00:03:53.600
this period of being the most technically
adept person in a given discipline.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.040
To a phase in which they're
actually adding value,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.089
not on the basis of their technical
expertise, because they're

00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:02.591
hiring people who have deeper expertise
in their particular discipline.

00:04:02.591 --> 00:04:07.561
And so the leader is not adding value by
telling that technical expert how to do

00:04:07.561 --> 00:04:09.472
their job or by, even worse,

00:04:09.472 --> 00:04:14.305
kind of shouldering that person out of
the way and doing their job for them.

00:04:14.305 --> 00:04:17.791
But by bringing a group of
technically adept people together and

00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:22.245
helping them operate as a team, ultimately
that distinction between leading and

00:04:22.245 --> 00:04:25.361
doing is how a leader truly
helps an organization scale.

00:04:25.361 --> 00:04:34.878
[MUSIC]

